response:
 First find a matrix that swaps the second row and the third row, then multiply it by $\mathbf{N}$. The matrix that swaps rows is \[\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\]. Then their product is
\begin{align*}
    \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\ d & e & f \\ g & h & i \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} d & e & f \\ g & h & i \\ b & a & c \end{pmatrix}\\
    &= \begin{pmatrix} d & e & f \\ a & b & c \\ 2i & 2h & 2g \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
but it didn't really double the third row, it actually made it equal to $2 \mathbf{N}$.  Now just multiply by $\frac{1}{2}$ on the left side, and you get \[\mathbf{M} \mathbf{N} = \frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} d & e & f \\ a & b & c \\ 2i & 2h & 2g \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{d}{2} & \frac{e}{2} & \frac{f}{2} \\ \frac{a}{2} & \frac{b}{2} & \frac{c}{2} \\ i & h & g \end{pmatrix}.\]Then, we get \[\mathbf{M} = \frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]</think> <answer> $\frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ </answer>

